[Event "Grünfeld: Exchange"]

{The Grünfeld (3...d5) lets White build a big centre and then attacks it with ...c5, ...Nc6 and the g7-bishop.}
1. d4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
1... Nf6 {Develops the knight to f6, where it controls e4 and d5.}
2. c4 {Space. Supports the centre: the pawn on c4 controls b5 and d5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
2... g6 {Fianchetto. The pawn on g6 controls f5 and h5.}
3. Nc3 {Develops the knight to c3, where it controls d5 and e4.}
3... d5 {[%cal Gd5c4] The Grünfeld. Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 controls e4 and eyes the pawn on c4. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8 and the queen on d8.}
4. cxd5 {The Exchange. Takes the pawn on d5; Black will want to recapture.}
4... Nxd5 {Recaptures on d5, restoring the material balance.}
5. e4 {Kicks the knight. Claims the centre: the pawn on e4 controls f5 and eyes the knight on d5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
5... Nxc3 {Takes the knight on c3; White will want to recapture.}
6. bxc3 {Recaptures toward the centre. Recaptures on c3, restoring the material balance.}
6... Bg7 {[%cal Gg7c3] Hits c3 and d4. Fianchettoes the bishop on g7, where it rakes the long diagonal through f6, e5 and d4. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
7. Bc4 {Develops the bishop to c4, where it controls d5.}
 (7. Nf3 {The modern Rb1 line. Develops the knight to f3, where it controls e5 and supports the pawn on d4.}
7... c5 {Hits d4. Supports the centre: the pawn on c5 controls b4 and eyes the pawn on d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
8. Rb1 {Prepares to take b7. The rook goes to b1.}
8... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
9. Be2 {Develops the bishop to e2, where it controls d3 and supports the knight on f3. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
9... cxd4 {Takes the pawn on d4; White will want to recapture.}
10. cxd4 {Recaptures on d4, restoring the material balance.}
10... Qa5+ {Checks. It is check, so the reply is forced. The queen goes to a5 and controls d5 and e5. It gives check and attacks the pawn on a2, which is undefended.}
11. Bd2 {Blocks. Develops the bishop to d2, where it controls e3 and f4. It attacks the queen on a5, which is undefended.}
11... Qxa2 {Takes a2. Takes the pawn on a2, and it cannot be taken back at once.}
12. O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
)
7... c5 {Hits d4. Supports the centre: the pawn on c5 controls b4 and eyes the pawn on d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
 (7... O-O {Castles first. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
8. Ne2 {Develops; f3 can support e4 later. The knight on e2 supports the pawn on d4. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
8... c5 {Now hits d4. Supports the centre: the pawn on c5 controls b4 and eyes the pawn on d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
)
8. Ne2 {Develops the knight to e2, where it supports the pawn on d4. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
8... Nc6 {More pressure on d4. Develops the knight to c6, where it controls e5 and eyes the pawn on d4.}
9. Be3 {Defends d4. Develops the bishop to e3, where it supports the pawn on d4.}
9... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
10. O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.} *

[Event "Grünfeld: 4.Bf4 & Russian"]

{Other main systems.}
1. d4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
1... Nf6 {Develops the knight to f6, where it controls e4 and d5.}
2. c4 {Space. Supports the centre: the pawn on c4 controls b5 and d5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
2... g6 {Fianchetto. The pawn on g6 controls f5 and h5.}
3. Nc3 {Develops the knight to c3, where it controls d5 and e4.}
3... d5 {Grünfeld. Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 controls e4 and eyes the pawn on c4. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8 and the queen on d8.}
4. Bf4 {Develops the bishop to f4, where it controls e5.}
 (4. Nf3 {Develops the knight to f3, where it controls e5 and supports the pawn on d4.}
4... Bg7 {Fianchettoes the bishop on g7, where it rakes the long diagonal through f6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
5. Qb3 {The Russian System. The queen goes to b3.}
5... dxc4 {Takes the pawn on c4; White will want to recapture.}
6. Qxc4 {Recaptures on c4, restoring the material balance.}
6... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
7. e4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on e4 controls d5 and f5. It also opens a line for the bishop on f1.}
7... a6 {The Hungarian. The pawn on a6 controls b5.}
)
4... Bg7 {Fianchettoes the bishop on g7, where it rakes the long diagonal through f6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
5. e3 {Supports the centre: the pawn on e3 supports the pawn on d4 and the bishop on f4. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
5... c5 {Hits d4. Supports the centre: the pawn on c5 controls b4 and eyes the pawn on d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
6. dxc5 {Takes the pawn on c5, and it cannot be taken back at once.}
6... Qa5 {Pins. The queen goes to a5. It attacks the pawn on c5, which is undefended.}
7. Rc1 {The rook goes to c1.}
7... dxc4 {Takes the pawn on c4; White will want to recapture.}
8. Bxc4 {Recaptures on c4, restoring the material balance.}
8... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.} *
